@Tutorial(time: 90) {
    @Intro(title: "Model Refactoring Pipeline") {
        Learn how to refactor a legacy metamodel and migrate its existing data with a repeatable pipeline of command-line tools.

        In this tutorial, you'll measure a legacy order metamodel and its data, find its structural and data-quality problems, design an improved metamodel with OCL invariants, migrate the legacy data with ATL transformations, check the results, and assemble a verified release with a rollback plan.

        @Image(source: "model-refactoring-pipeline.svg", alt: "The refactoring pipeline: legacy exports are cleaned by a refining transformation, migrated to the improved metamodel, validated against its invariants, checked by quality assurance and assembled into a verified release.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Analysing Legacy Models") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Refactoring starts with understanding the current model. You'll analyse the order system of a small electronics store: a metamodel that has grown over the years and the monthly order exports written with it.

            Size metrics show where the structure is overloaded, and queries over the data show the quality problems that the old structure allows. Together they decide what the refactoring must change. All files of this tutorial live in a `refactoring` directory.

            @Image(source: "legacy-model-analysis.svg", alt: "Legacy model analysis: the metamodel and the data are validated, measured with statistics and inspect, and queried with AQL; the findings feed the refactoring plan.")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Examine the legacy metamodel.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/LegacyOrders.ecore", file: workflow-02-step-01-legacy-metamodel.ecore)

                One `Order` class holds the order, the customer's details, two addresses as text, the payment and stored totals. Statuses and payment methods are free text, and items name their products by a code string.
            }

            @Step {
                Add the January export of the legacy system.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/legacy/orders-2024-01.xmi", file: workflow-02-step-02-legacy-instances.xmi)

                Five orders show the problems that the structure allows: the same status in different cases, a repeat customer typed in again with a different spelling, a product code in lower case and a stored total that no longer matches its items.
            }

            @Step {
                Measure the metamodel and the data.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-03-measure-legacy.sh)

                `swift-ecore statistics` counts the classes and features of the metamodel and ranks the classes by size: `Order` has 15 features, 14 of them attributes. `inspect --show-attributes` shows what they are. For a model, the statistics count the objects per class.
            }

            @Step {
                Find data-quality problems with AQL.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-04-find-data-issues.sh)

                Each query checks one suspicion from the metamodel: inconsistent status spellings, customers stored more than once, product codes without a product, and stored totals that disagree with their items.
            }

            @Step {
                Write down the findings and the refactoring goals.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/refactoring-plan.md", file: workflow-02-step-05-refactoring-plan.txt)

                The plan turns each finding into a goal and states measurable success criteria, which the later steps check one by one.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Designing the Target Model") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The improved metamodel addresses each finding of the analysis: customers, addresses, payments and stock levels become classes of their own, free text becomes enumerations, and items refer to products.

            Rules that the legacy data broke become OCL invariants of the metamodel, so that every migrated model can be checked automatically.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Design the improved metamodel.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/Orders.ecore", file: workflow-02-step-06-improved-metamodel.ecore)

                The new namespace URI marks version 2.0. Totals are no longer stored; they are computed from the items. The invariants require unique order numbers, customer e-mail addresses and product codes, e-mail addresses in lower case with an `@`, and positive prices and quantities. Multiplicities state the simpler rules: every order has a status, a customer, a shipping address, a payment with a method, and at least one item (`items` is `1..*`).
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the design and compare it with the legacy metamodel.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-07-validate-and-compare.sh)

                The largest class now has 9 features instead of 15. `swift-ecore compare` lists the classes, features and enumerations that were added, removed or changed between the two versions; a renamed feature such as `desc` appears as one removed and one added feature. This list is the basis of the mapping.
            }

            @Step {
                Document how legacy concepts map to the improved ones.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/legacy-to-improved-mapping.md", file: workflow-02-step-08-mapping.txt)

                The mapping names the transformation rule for every legacy feature and says which features are deliberately not migrated, such as stored totals and `lastModified`.
            }

            @Step {
                Plan the compatibility strategy.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/compatibility-strategy.md", file: workflow-02-step-09-compatibility-strategy.txt)

                Both versions coexist during the transition: the legacy exports stay unchanged, tools that still read version 1.0 get exports written back in the legacy format, and a checked backup allows a rollback.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Creating Migration Transformations") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            ATL transformations migrate the legacy data to the improved structure. A refining transformation first cleans the data in place, then the migration restructures it, and the invariants of the improved metamodel check the result.

            A backward transformation writes migrated models in the legacy format again. Comparing its output with the cleaned data shows exactly what the migration changed.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the migration transformation.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/LegacyOrders2Orders.atl", file: workflow-02-step-10-migration-transformation.atl)

                A unique lazy rule creates one `Customer` per e-mail address from the customer's first order. Helpers split address text into its parts and turn status and payment text into enumeration literals, and items are linked to the product with their code.
            }

            @Step {
                Add a data-quality transformation that runs before the migration.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/CleanLegacyOrders.atl", file: workflow-02-step-11-data-quality-refining.atl)

                The module is declared with `refining`: its output starts as a copy of the legacy export, and its rules only normalise the case and surrounding spaces of statuses, payment methods, e-mail addresses and product codes.
            }

            @Step {
                Test the transformations with the January export.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-12-test-transformations.sh)

                The migrated model passes validation, including every invariant. `compare --summary` counts the objects per class in both files: orders, items and products keep their numbers, and four customers replace the copied customer details.
            }

            @Step {
                See why the cleaning step is needed.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-13-validation-catches-raw-data.sh)

                Migrating the raw export creates the repeat customer twice, once with a mixed-case e-mail address, and leaves the item with code `cable-001` without a product. Two orders spell their status and payment method in lower or mixed case, so they match no literal and the required `status` and `method` stay unset. As in the Eclipse Modeling Framework, an unset enumeration reads as its first literal and passes a lower bound of 1 by default; `--require-explicit-values` asks every required feature for a value of its own, which suits migrated data. Validation rejects the model, so such problems cannot slip through unnoticed.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the backward transformation for tools that still read version 1.0.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/Orders2LegacyOrders.atl", file: workflow-02-step-14-backward-transformation.atl)

                It copies the customer, address and payment details into each order again and computes the stored totals from the items, rounded to cents.
            }

            @Step {
                Check what the migration changed.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-15-check-compatibility.sh)

                Writing the migrated model back and comparing it with the cleaned export leaves four differences, all in ORD-2024-004: its stored total and tax were wrong in the legacy data and are now computed from the items, and its addresses gain the country that the migration fills in. Every other value survived the migration.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Batch Instance Migration") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With tested transformations, every legacy export can be migrated in one run. Scripts make the migration repeatable: they process each file, skip files that are already done, and check every result.

            Reports and a checksum manifest record what the migration produced.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Collect further legacy exports.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-16-collect-legacy-exports.sh)

                The February and March exports have their own problems: a status with a trailing space, payment methods in mixed case, and a repeat customer whose e-mail address starts with capitals. `swift-ecore validate` checks all three files at once.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the migration script.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/migrate-all-instances.sh", file: workflow-02-step-17-migrate-all-instances.sh)

                For each legacy export, the script runs the cleaning and the migration transformation. A file that already has a migrated model is skipped, so an interrupted run can simply be started again; deleting a migrated model makes the next run migrate that export again.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the validation script.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/validate-migration.sh", file: workflow-02-step-18-validate-migration.sh)

                The script validates all migrated models in one call, then compares the numbers of orders, items, products and customers of each model with its cleaned export, and fails if any count differs.
            }

            @Step {
                Run the batch migration.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-19-run-batch-migration.sh)

                All three exports are migrated and every check passes. Running the migration again skips every file, because nothing has changed.
            }

            @Step {
                Generate the migration reports.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-20-generate-reports.sh)

                For each model, `swift-ecore statistics` and `swift-ecore compare --summary` write JSON reports, and `shasum` records a checksum manifest of the migrated files that can be verified at any time.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Quality Assurance and Rollout") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Before the migrated models replace the legacy ones, a quality-assurance script checks them once more, from strict validation to a round trip through another format. The tools that depend on the models are updated, and a backup with checksums makes the change reversible.

            The result is a release directory whose contents a consumer can verify independently.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the quality-assurance script.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/comprehensive-qa.sh", file: workflow-02-step-21-comprehensive-qa.sh)

                Each check computes its result: strict validation, AQL assertions that every item has a product and that statuses and item amounts are unchanged, and an XMI to JSON to XMI round trip that `swift-ecore compare --exit-code` must find identical.
            }

            @Step {
                Run the quality assurance.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-22-run-qa.sh)

                Every check passes for all three models. A failing check would be reported with its expected and actual value, and the script would exit with an error.
            }

            @Step {
                Rewrite the order report for the improved metamodel.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/OrderSummary.mtl", file: workflow-02-step-23-order-summary.mtl)

                Tools that read the models must follow the new structure: the report takes customer names from `Customer` objects and computes order totals from the items instead of reading a stored total.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the script that updates the dependent tools.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/update-toolchain.sh", file: workflow-02-step-24-update-toolchain.sh)

                It regenerates the Swift model classes from `Orders.ecore`, the order summaries from the migrated models, and legacy-format exports for the tools that still read version 1.0.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the backup and rollback procedures.

                @Code(name: "refactoring/rollback-procedures.sh", file: workflow-02-step-25-rollback-procedures.sh)

                The backup copies the legacy models and their metamodel together with a SHA-256 checksum list. A restore verifies the checksums first, so a damaged backup is never restored, and then validates the restored models.
            }

            @Step {
                Assemble, verify and release the refactored models.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-02-step-26-release.sh)

                The release holds the improved metamodel, the migrated models and their checksums, and is verified as a consumer would verify it. A rehearsed restore proves that the rollback brings back the legacy models byte for byte.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            What should be the first step in model refactoring?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Analyse the legacy model and its data to identify problems

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    Metrics of the metamodel and queries over the data show where the structure is overloaded and which quality problems it allows, which gives the refactoring concrete goals.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Start creating the improved metamodel immediately

                @Justification(reaction: "Too hasty!") {
                    Without analysing the legacy model and data first, you might miss problems such as repeated customer details or wrong stored totals.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Begin migrating instances to a new format

                @Justification(reaction: "No target to migrate to!") {
                    You need to analyse the legacy model and design the target model before you can write migration transformations.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Why does the pipeline run a refining transformation before the migration?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Because ATL cannot change the case of strings during a migration

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The migration could normalise strings as well. Keeping the clean-up in its own step makes each transformation simpler and lets you check the cleaned data separately.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                It normalises the legacy data, so that the migration can rely on consistent statuses, e-mail addresses and product codes

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly!") {
                    Without cleaning, a repeat customer becomes two customers and an item loses its product, as the validation of the raw migration shows.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It converts the legacy model to the improved metamodel

                @Justification(reaction: "That is the migration's job!") {
                    A refining transformation keeps the metamodel of its input. It only changes the values its rules assign.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Why is backwards compatibility important during model refactoring?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It prevents any changes from being made to the model

                @Justification(reaction: "That would defeat the purpose!") {
                    Backwards compatibility allows necessary changes while minimising disruption. The goal is controlled evolution, not stagnation.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                It minimises disruption to tools and processes that still use the old format

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly!") {
                    A backward transformation and a checked backup let the tools move to the new version one at a time, and allow a rollback if something goes wrong.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                It's only important for large-scale refactoring projects

                @Justification(reaction: "Important for all projects!") {
                    Even small refactoring efforts benefit from considering backwards compatibility to avoid unexpected disruptions.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            How should you validate model refactoring results?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Only check that the new metamodel is well-formed

                @Justification(reaction: "Much more validation needed!") {
                    A well-formed metamodel says nothing about whether the data survived the migration.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Combine several checks: validation with invariants, object counts, value comparisons and round trips

                @Justification(reaction: "Comprehensive approach!") {
                    Each check catches different problems: invariants catch broken rules, counts catch lost objects, comparisons catch changed values, and round trips catch serialisation problems.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Rely on a manual review of a few migrated files

                @Justification(reaction: "Not repeatable!") {
                    A manual review is useful, but automated checks run on every file and every time the migration is repeated.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
